{{box title="Status of this page"}}
Objectives and Functions are drafted and traceable to the Foundation.

On evidence. This project has collected no primary data: no resident or care worker has been observed or interviewed. Each Evidence basis field below says so, and cites published literature instead. Where nothing could be found the field says TODO. Full citations are in the References section at the bottom, each marked with how confident we are in it.

Technology Options are selected in §1.3 (Pepper, TECH-01, with item sensors, TECH-02, and a staff notification channel, TECH-03). The //Escalation specification// below gives the prompt ladder per step, its timing, and the handover rules; its values and the Premise thresholds are proposals, to be confirmed in the first EM-01 pilot (O-20). Measures and Evaluation Methods cited below are documented on their §1.2 Foundation pages. A first cognitive walkthrough of UC01 (ER-01, §3) has been done on the specification; its findings are Open items O-25 to O-30, and the summative study design for EM-02 is ER-02. See //Open items//.
{{/box}}

= Objective  [OBJ-01] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification. Per the verbatim Function definition, a Function is specified to contribute to an operational objective, a human-factors objective, or a Human Value, so this is where Foundation knowledge becomes something a Function can be traced to.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-01
|Name|Activity reaches completion without care-worker prompting carrying it
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from the Goal recorded on SA-01, and from PS-01's account of how that Goal currently fails.

Literature: Rogers et al. (1999) found that systematic graduated prompting of nursing-home residents with dementia produced a 26% increase in self-dressing and significantly fewer physical assists than usual care, in a severely impaired cohort (mean MMSE 6.07). That establishes the effect direction is achievable.

No primary data collected by this project. SA-01's own Evidence basis rests on literature and group reasoning only.
)))
|Statement|The resident reaches the last step of the tooth-brushing activity, and the verbal prompting a care worker currently has to supply is no longer what carries them there.
|Type|Operational
|Derived from|Goal on SA-01 ("maintain oral hygiene as part of the resident's normal morning routine, with as much independence as possible")
|Priority|Must. This is the Goal SA-01 states, and the point at which PS-01 breaks down.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → Goal (SA-01); served-by → F1, F2, F3; referenced-by → UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F3-CL6.

= Objective  [OBJ-02] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-02
|Name|A genuine opportunity to act before assistance arrives
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-01 and HV-04, and from VT-03's current resolution.

Literature: Self-determination theory (Ryan & Deci, 2000) for the value itself. For why this matters specifically in dementia care, Kitwood (1997) names //disempowerment// - "not allowing or enabling a person to use the abilities they still have" - as one of the detractors that erode personhood.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|At each step of the activity, the resident has had a real opportunity to act on their own before any assistance arrives.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-01 Autonomy and HV-04 Competence. Informed by VT-03's resolution, which already states the trade-off this Objective sits on.
|Priority|Must. Without it, OBJ-01 could be satisfied by a System that simply narrates every step - which is what the existing workaround recorded on SA-01 already does, and what PS-01 identifies as the problem rather than the solution.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-01, HV-04; served-by → F2; referenced-by → UC01-F2-CL1, UC01-F2-CL3, UC01-F2-CL4; narrated-by → OS-01.

= Objective  [OBJ-03] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-03
|Name|Difficulty resolved where it appears, before it becomes distress
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HFC-03.

Literature: de Werd et al. (2013) review error-prevention techniques for teaching everyday tasks to people with dementia; Rogers et al. (1999) found disruptive behaviours //decreased// significantly under systematic prompting. Both support the principle that assistance timing matters to distress.

Contested on direction - the same literature favours preventing the error rather than allowing it, which is not what F2 does. See Open item O-7.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|Where the resident cannot continue unaided, the difficulty is resolved at the step where it appears, rather than accumulating into confusion or distress.
|Type|Human-factors
|Derived from|HFC-03 Adaptive assistance, competence and distress
|Priority|Must. This is the other side of OBJ-02; VT-03 is the trade-off between the two, and a design that serves only one of them has not resolved that tension.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HFC-03; served-by → F2, F3; referenced-by → UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F2-CL4, UC01-F3-CL6.

= Objective  [OBJ-04] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-04
|Name|Support without the feeling of being watched
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-03, and from the scope decision recorded in PS-02's out-of-scope field ("we only report a failure in completing the task, we avoid surveilling the patient in general").

Literature: Niemeijer et al. (2010), a systematic review of 79 papers on the ethical and practical acceptability of surveillance technology in residential care for people with dementia. Niemeijer et al. (2015) then asked residents themselves, and concluded such technology can contribute to autonomy //only// when embedded in a person-centred approach - which is the condition this Objective encodes.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The resident is supported through the activity without experiencing it as being watched, and without detail about a personal-care activity reaching anyone who does not need it.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-03 Privacy. Bounded by VT-02's resolution, and explained by HFC-02.
|Priority|Should. It constrains how F1 and F4 may be built rather than adding a capability of its own - but the constraint is hard, because tooth brushing is a personal-care activity.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-03; served-by → F1, F3, F4; referenced-by → UC01-F1-CL7, UC01-F4-CL8; narrated-by → OS-02.

= Objective  [OBJ-05] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-05
|Name|Prompting that does not talk down to the resident
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-02's recognition criteria, which names infantilisation explicitly.

Literature: Kitwood (1997) lists //infantilisation// - "treating the person like a child; using condescending language or gestures toward an adult" - among the 17 detractors of personhood that make up what he called malignant social psychology in dementia care. //Outpacing// (giving information or choices too quickly) is a second detractor directly relevant to how F2 paces a prompt.

Robot-specific support: Lee et al. (2016) on ageing stereotypes embedded in assistive robot design; Coghlan et al. (2021), whose older participants called companion robots "patronizing" when they were toy-like or imitated human companionship; Furlanis et al. (2026) for why an embodied system carries this risk differently from a screen, via their "embodied treatment" dimension - gaze, timing, tone. See Open item O-10.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The resident is prompted through the activity without being addressed as less capable than they are.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-02 Dignity
|Priority|Should. Distinct from OBJ-02: OBJ-02 is about whether the opportunity to act exists, this is about how the prompt reads when it does arrive. A System could serve one and violate the other.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-02; served-by → F2; referenced-by → UC01-F2-CL5.

= Objective  [OBJ-06] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-06
|Name|The care worker's time goes where it is needed, and care continues at handover
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from the Goal recorded on SA-03 (the care worker's morning round), added to close Open item O-3 and O-14.

Literature: Rogers et al. (1999) found that graduated prompting of nursing-home residents with dementia took considerably more caregiver time than usual care - the time cost this Objective asks the System to absorb.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The care worker is only pulled back to a resident when prompting has run out, the resident is distressed, or there is a safety risk, and afterwards knows what support each resident needed.
|Type|Operational
|Derived from|Goal on SA-03 ("get every resident on the round through their morning care safely and in time, giving each the support they actually need, and know afterwards what each resident needed"). Serves HV-06 Attentive care; bounded by VT-07.
|Priority|Should. It is the project's stated aim of relieving care staff, but it must not be met at the expense of OBJ-01 to OBJ-03: a System that saves staff time by letting activities go unfinished fails.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → Goal (SA-03), HV-06; served-by → F3, F4; referenced-by → UC01-F3-CL9, UC01-F4-CL8.

= Use Case  [UC01] =

//A formal description of one distinct Goal-directed interaction between Actors and the System. One Use Case per interaction, not per screen or per Technology feature.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC01
|Name|Support the resident through the morning tooth-brushing routine
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Formalizes SA-01 and PS-01.

Closest prior system: COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008) prompted six older adults with moderate-to-severe dementia through handwashing using audio and audio-video prompts, and scored what M-02 scores here - one point per step completed without human assistance. It is the nearest published precedent for this Use Case and should be cited wherever this design is defended.

No primary data collected by this project. No resident or care worker has been observed or interviewed; SA-01 and PS-01 rest on literature plus the group's own reasoning.
)))
|Objective(s) served|OBJ-01, OBJ-02, OBJ-03, OBJ-04, OBJ-05, OBJ-06
|TDP(s) and IDP(s) used|TDP-01 Graduated activity support with human handover (§2.4) allocates the tasks between resident, System and care worker; IDP-01 Respectful graduated prompting (§2.5) shapes F2's prompts. Both are drafted from UC01 alone. Confirming that they recur needs UC02 (see UC02's scope decision).
|Actors|(((
ST-01 Resident with dementia (Direct) - the only Actor who interacts with the System in real time.

ST-02 Care home staff member (Direct) - receives handover from F3 and reviews the F4 record; does not operate the System during the activity.

The System - Pepper (TECH-01) for prompts and interaction, sensors on the tooth-brushing items (TECH-02) for tracking, and a staff notification channel and session record (TECH-03). See §1.3.

ST-03 Family members (Indirect) - affected by outcomes, not an Actor in this Use Case, and not a recipient of anything F4 records.
)))
|Trigger|The resident's tooth-brushing window opens - SA-01's trigger, after waking, washing and/or breakfast - and either the resident is at the sink, or the window is running down with the activity not started. Both are in scope, because PS-01's first sub-problem is initiation.
|Pre-condition|(((
The resident's SA-01 step sequence is configured, together with the assistance baseline they currently need.

The item sensors (TECH-02) are fitted and reporting, and Pepper (TECH-01) is charged and positioned at the bathroom doorway, within earshot and sight of the sink, without blocking the resident's path. Pepper's cameras are disabled and visibly covered.

A care worker is reachable within the home for F3.

Consent arrangements for ST-01 are in place, on the terms VT-02's resolution sets.
)))
|Post-condition|(((
One of the following holds:

- the resident has completed SA-01 step 9, unaided or after prompting at some level;

- a care worker has taken over following F3;

- the window has closed with the activity incomplete, and that fact is recorded.

In every case F4 holds a session record, and no step-by-step trace of the activity is retained.
)))
|Action Sequence|(((
~1. The tooth-brushing window opens. The System begins tracking the activity (F1). **Deliberate addition** - SA-01 task 1 is the resident recognising that it is time, which PS-01 shows failing.

2. The resident enters the bathroom and begins (SA-01 tasks 2-3).

2-alt. The resident has not begun by the end of the window. F2 issues an initiation prompt. If there is no response, step 9.

3. The resident performs a step; the System tracks progress (F1). SA-01 tasks 4-8.

3-alt. During SA-01 task 7, brushing stops before the resident's target brushing time, or the same item event repeats without the next step following. Either is treated as a stall on the current step; go to step 4. The early-stop case has its own limits, set out in the //Escalation specification//.

4. A stall is detected on the current step. The System waits the configured interval and does nothing. **Deliberate addition** - the wait is the design, not an implementation delay. See OS-01, and Open item O-7 for the literature that contests it.

5. The resident resumes unaided. Return to step 3.

6. The stall persists past the interval. F2 issues the lowest-level prompt available for that step.

7. The resident resumes. F2 stops prompting; return to step 3.

8. The stall persists. F2 re-prompts one level more specific, up to the demonstration level.

8-alt. Signs of distress at any point. Remaining prompt levels are skipped; go to step 9.

9. Prompting is exhausted without progress. F3 requests care-worker assistance, passing the room, the activity, the step reached, and the reason.

10. A safety-critical condition occurs at any point - an immediate risk of serious harm, inferred from no sensor change and no response to prompts for a configured time (TECH-02 limitation). F3 immediately, pre-empting any prompting, per VT-01's current resolution. **Deliberate addition** - in scope by group decision (Open item O-2).

11. The resident completes SA-01 step 9, or a care worker takes over, or the window closes. F4 records the session. **Deliberate addition** relative to SA-01; it corresponds to the care worker's own recording and handover task (SA-03 task 6).

**Exception handling.** If the System cannot track the activity - sensor loss, power, or an unrecognised sequence - it reports a degraded state to the care worker rather than failing silently, and the activity falls back to the existing SA-01 workarounds. Verified by UC01-F1-PR1.
)))
|Function(s) realized|F1, F2, F3, F4 - specified in full below.

== Functions ==

**Function F1** (track position in the activity sequence): the System determines which step of the SA-01 sequence the resident is currently on, and whether progress to the next step has stalled, from the resident's observable actions on the activity's own tools and fixtures - requiring the resident neither to report anything nor to operate the System. During SA-01 task 7 it also sums the time the toothbrush is in brushing motion, which it uses within the session and does not keep (VT-08). It observes the activity only, not the resident in general. Serves OBJ-01, OBJ-04. Bounded by VT-02, VT-05. Enabled by TECH-02 (item sensors) - deliberately not by Pepper's cameras (TECH-04 rejected).

**Function F2** (prompt at the lowest sufficient level, after a wait): on a detected stall, the System waits a configured interval, then issues the least specific prompt that could unblock the current step. If the stall persists it re-prompts one level more specific - orienting the resident to the activity, then naming the next action, then demonstrating it - and stops as soon as the resident resumes. If brushing stops before the resident's target brushing time, F2 offers once to continue; this offer never leads to a handover on its own. Prompts address the resident by name and in the second person, phrased as an offer rather than an instruction. Serves OBJ-01, OBJ-02, OBJ-03, OBJ-05. Bounded by VT-01, VT-03, VT-04, VT-05. Enabled by TECH-01 (Pepper): spoken prompts for the orienting and naming levels, and a picture or short video on its chest tablet for the demonstration level, rather than miming with its arms (VT-04 mitigation).

//Where F2's ladder comes from. It is not invented: it is the escalation SA-01 already records as existing workarounds - verbal reminder, then naming, then demonstrating the brushing action, then physical assistance - made systematic, with physical assistance deliberately left to the care worker via F3 rather than taken on by the System. Rogers et al. (1999) is the published precedent for graduated prompting of this kind in nursing-home dementia care.//

//Where it is contested. F2 is a least-to-most ladder. The dementia ADL literature largely runs the other way, favouring errorless learning and vanishing cues - prompt early to prevent the error, then fade support across sessions (de Werd et al., 2013). The largest trial of that approach, REDALI-DEM, then found structured relearning improved ADL performance but errorless learning gave no advantage over trial-and-error learning. So the evidence neither endorses F2's direction nor rules it out.//

//**Design bet (group decision, Open item O-7).** F2 keeps its least-to-most direction as a deliberate bet, not an established finding: we expect the wait-then-escalate ladder to preserve competence (OBJ-02) without causing avoidable frustration. UC01-F2-CL4 is the adverse Claim designed to settle it. If CL4 holds, F2 is re-specified - first with a per-resident wait interval, and if that is not enough, as most-to-least with fading.//

**Function F3** (hand over to a care worker): the System requests care-worker assistance when prompting has been exhausted without progress, when the resident shows signs of distress, or when a safety-critical condition occurs. It passes only the resident's room, the activity, the step reached, and the reason for handover. The triggers, priorities, acknowledgement and re-escalation rules are in the //Escalation specification//. Serves OBJ-01, OBJ-03, OBJ-04, OBJ-06. Bounded by VT-01 - whose current resolution includes the safety-critical path - and by VT-02, VT-07. Enabled by TECH-03 (notification to the care worker's work phone), with TECH-01 telling the resident that someone is coming.

**Function F4** (record the session for handover): the System records, per session, whether the activity was completed, the highest prompt level required, and whether a care worker was called. It retains no step-by-step detail of the activity itself. Serves OBJ-04, OBJ-06. Bounded by VT-02. Enabled by TECH-03 (session record), from TECH-02 data.

//Note: F4 traces to OBJ-06, derived from the care worker's own Goal on SA-03 (care continuity at handover). Previously it had no Objective; see Open item O-3.//

== Escalation specification ==

//Answers the midterm feedback to say in more detail how and when support escalates. It makes F1's stall rule, F2's ladder and F3's handover concrete enough to build and to test in EM-01. Every value below is a proposed starting value from group reasoning, not a finding: none has been tried with a resident or a care worker. Each one is configurable per resident, and the defaults are to be confirmed in the first EM-01 pilot and with care workers (Open item O-20).//

=== Timing parameters ===

|=Parameter|=Controls|=Proposed default|=Per-resident range|=Basis
|T-stall|Time without the next expected item event before F1 declares a stall (Action Sequence step 4)|20 s|15 to 45 s|SA-01 records 2 to 5 minutes per episode. About two minutes of that is brushing, so each of the other steps normally takes in the order of 10 to 30 s. Twenty seconds without the expected event is therefore long for a normal step, and short enough for the whole ladder to fit inside one episode.
|T-pause|Pause in brushing motion that counts as brushing having stopped, during SA-01 task 7|10 s|5 to 20 s|Short pauses to spit or rinse the brush are part of normal brushing and must not trigger a prompt (VT-05).
|W|Silent wait after a detected stall, before the first prompt (step 4)|15 s|0 to 60 s|The wait is the design (OS-01). W is the first value to change per resident if UC01-F2-CL4 holds; W = 0 is the O-7 fallback for a resident who needs prompting at once.
|R1, R2|Response window after an orienting (L1) or naming (L2) prompt, before the next level|20 s|15 to 40 s|Long enough to hear one short sentence and act on it. A shorter window would risk outpacing, which HFC-04 names as a detractor.
|R3|Response window after the demonstration (L3)|30 s|20 to 60 s|The picture or clip takes longer to take in than a sentence.
|N-repeat|Times the same item event repeats without the next expected event before it counts as a stall|3|2 to 5|SA-01 records performing a single step over and over as a breakdown. TECH-02 sees it as the same pick-up and put-down repeating.
|B-target|Total brushing time that counts as SA-01 task 7 done|120 s|60 to 180 s, set with ST-05 or a dental professional|The commonly recommended two minutes. Seuntjens et al. (2025) meta-analysed single brushing exercises and found two minutes removed more plaque than one, with a medium effect for manual brushes (SMD 0.69) and a small one for powered brushes (SMD 0.47), and rated the recommendation moderate certainty. Their trials did not involve care-home residents with dementia, so per-resident targets are still agreed with ST-05 or a dental professional (Open item O-23).
|T-safe|Time with no item event and no spoken or touch response, once the activity has started, before the safety-critical path (step 10)|180 s|120 to 300 s|DS-01 speaks of several minutes. T-safe only starts after the first item event of the session, because silence before that is normal. With the defaults the ladder runs out about 105 s after the last event, so T-safe comes after a routine handover that has had no response.

With the defaults, a resident who stops and does not respond hears L1 about 35 s after their last action, L2 at 55 s, sees L3 at 75 s, and the care worker is called at about 105 s.

=== Prompt levels ===

|=Level|=M-03 code|=What the resident gets|=Channel (TECH-01)
|L0 Wait|0|Nothing. Pepper stays silent with dimmed eye lights.|None
|L1 Orienting|1|A sentence about the activity, without naming the action: "Mary, you are brushing your teeth this morning."|Speech
|L2 Naming|2|The next action, phrased as an offer (step-specific wording in the next table).|Speech
|L3 Demonstration|3|The L2 sentence once more, plus a picture or short clip of the step on the chest tablet. The picture stays on screen until the step is done or the care worker arrives.|Speech and tablet
|Handover|4|F3 (see the handover rules below). Pepper tells the resident that someone is coming.|Speech; message to the care worker's phone (TECH-03)

Ladder rules:

~1. One level at a time, each given once, with the response window between levels. This is what UC01-F2-PR2 verifies.

2. Any expected item event counts as resuming. Prompting stops, and the next stall, on whatever step, starts again at L1.

3. If the resident says no to two prompts in a row, or says stop, prompting stops and F3 sends a routine handover with the reason "declined". Speech the System does not understand counts as no reply, because F2 must not depend on speech recognition (TECH-01).

4. If the resident asks for help or says they are upset, the remaining levels are skipped (step 8-alt) and F3 sends a priority handover. TECH-02 cannot see distress, so this is the System's weakest detection. Distress the System misses is what the observers measure for UC01-F2-CL4 (M-04).

5. The early-stop case at SA-01 task 7 gets L1 and L2 only, once per session, and never leads to a handover on its own. A resident who stops brushing early and does not take up the offer moves on to rinsing. Calling a care worker over brushing time alone would cost HV-06 and HV-01 more than the extra brushing gains (VT-08).

=== Ladder per step ===

|=SA-01 step|=Progress signal (TECH-02)|=Stall rule|=L2 naming cue (L3 shows the same step on the tablet)|=Notes
|1 to 3. Recognise it is time, go to the sink, locate the items|First lift on the toothbrush cup or toothpaste pad|Initiation path (step 2-alt): no lift when 10 minutes of the window remain|L1: "Good morning Mary, it is time for your teeth." L2: "Would you like to come and brush your teeth?"|Pepper stands at the bathroom doorway, so a resident elsewhere in the room may not hear it (O-21). No lift by the time the window closes: routine handover, reason "not started".
|4. Pick up toothbrush|Toothbrush motion sensor: picked up|T-stall after the first lift, or N-repeat|"Would you like to pick up your toothbrush?"|
|5 and 6. Open toothpaste, apply it|Toothpaste lifted from its pad and returned while the brush is held|T-stall after the brush is picked up|"Would you like some toothpaste on your brush?"|TECH-02 cannot confirm paste is on the brush. A lift and return is taken as done.
|7. Brush all teeth|Brushing motion; total brushing time counted toward B-target|No brushing motion T-stall after the toothpaste is returned; or brushing stops for T-pause before B-target (early stop)|"You could brush your teeth now." Early stop: "Would you like to brush a little longer?"|Early stop follows ladder rule 5. Brushing time is used within the session only and is not recorded (F4, VT-08).
|8. Rinse and spit|Tap flow sensor, or cup lifted|T-stall after brushing ends|"Would you like to rinse your mouth?"|
|9. Put things away|Toothbrush back on the cup pad, toothpaste back on its pad|T-stall after rinsing|"Would you like to put the brush back in the cup?"|If the ladder runs out here, nobody is called: the record marks the session incomplete and the care worker sees it at handover.

=== Handover rules (F3) ===

Every message carries the same four items, the resident's room, the activity, the step reached and the reason, plus a priority. The room was added after the ER-01 walkthrough found that a care worker with several residents on the round could not tell where to go (WF-07, Open item O-25); it goes only to the care worker who is already responsible for that resident. The priority describes the message, so it adds nothing about the resident to what F4 or TECH-03 holds.

|=Code|=Condition|=Priority|=Reason sent|=Acknowledgement and re-escalation (TECH-03)
|H1|Ladder exhausted on steps 4 to 8; initiation path ended with nothing started; resident declined twice|Routine|"no progress", "not started" or "declined"|Not acknowledged within 5 min: re-sent once to the same care worker. Not acknowledged within 10 min: sent to a second care worker.
|H2|Resident asks for help or says they are upset|Priority|"asked for help"|Not acknowledged within 2 min: sent to a second care worker.
|H3|T-safe elapsed with no item event and no response (step 10)|Urgent. Pre-empts any prompting.|"no response, possible risk"|Re-sent every 60 s. Not acknowledged within 60 s: sent to a second care worker as well, and through the home's nurse-call system where it can be connected (TECH-03 limitation).
|D|The System cannot track the activity: sensor loss, Pepper unavailable, or an unrecognised sequence (exception path)|Information|"System not tracking"|Sent once per session, never re-sent. The care worker falls back to the SA-01 workarounds.

Rules that keep calls rare and meaningful, as HP-02 asks and HFC-06 explains:

- At most one routine handover per resident per session. After H1 the System stops prompting and waits for the care worker.

- No message for an early stop at SA-01 task 7, or for a ladder that runs out at task 9.

- If more than half of a resident's sessions in a week end in H1, the care team reviews that resident's W and response windows, or whether the System suits them at all. At that rate the System is adding calls, not taking them away (VT-07, UC01-F3-CL9).

The priorities and the escalation to a second care worker follow two of the alarm-management recommendations in Cvach (2012): alarm priority levels, and notification systems that can delay and escalate. Sending no message for an early stop at task 7 or a ladder that runs out at task 9 follows its advice to send an alert only when it calls for action.

== Premises table ==

One row per Premise. Verification only - does the Function operate as specified.

|=Premise ID|=Function|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Verification Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC01-F1-PR1|F1|EM-01, M-01, M-08 (brushing-time component). No human subjects.|(((
Accuracy and robustness. The System identifies the resident's current SA-01 step correctly for at least 90% of staged steps, and detects a stall within T-stall + 5 s of its onset (25 s with the defaults). Repeated item events are recognised as a stall at the N-repeat count. During SA-01 task 7 the summed brushing time is within 10 s of the ground-truth log per session. Proposed values, O-20.

On sensor loss or an unrecognised sequence, a degraded state is reported to the care worker rather than the System failing silently.
)))|1, 3, 3-alt, 4, exception path
|UC01-F2-PR2|F2|EM-01, M-01 (timing component). No human subjects.|(((
Timing and conformity to specified interaction behaviour. The full wait interval elapses before any prompt is issued. Prompting ascends exactly one level at a time, never skipping a level except on the distress path. Prompting stops within 2 s of the resident resuming. W and each response window are kept to within 2 s of their configured values. No prompt is repeated at the same level more than once. Spurious prompts, given when no stall occurred, stay at or below 1 per 10 staged sessions. The early-stop offer at SA-01 task 7 is given at most once per session and never leads to a handover. Proposed values, O-20.

The false-prompt rate matters more than it looks: mistimed prompts were the failure mode observed in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008), and are the mechanism by which UC01-F2-CL4 would come true.
)))|4, 6, 7, 8
|UC01-F3-PR3|F3|EM-01, M-01. No human subjects.|(((
Timing, correctness, and availability. A handover request reaches a care worker within 10 s of the triggering condition (H1, H2), and within 5 s for the safety-critical path (H3). The request carries only room, activity, step reached, and reason, with its priority. The safety-critical path pre-empts all pending prompting and does not queue behind it. In every staged case where nobody acknowledges, the re-send and second-care-worker escalation happen at the configured times in the handover rules. Proposed values, O-20.
)))|9, 10, 8-alt
|UC01-F4-PR4|F4|EM-01, M-06 (record-content component). No human subjects.|(((
Correctness and conformity. The session record contains exactly the three specified items and no step-level trace of the activity. Records are not readable by roles outside the agreed set - ST-03 in particular is not a recipient.
)))|11

== Claims table ==

One row per Claim. Validation - does the Function produce the intended effect. Premise and Claim numbers are drawn from project-wide counters, not reset per Use Case or per Function.

|=Claim ID|=Function|=Serves Objective|=Effect|=Grounding|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Validation Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC01-F2-CL1|F2|OBJ-02, OBJ-01|Positive. Increases the number of SA-01 steps the resident completes with no prompt at all, compared with the care worker's current prompt-on-hesitation pattern. Concerns ST-01, SA-01, PS-01.|HFC-03; HV-01, HV-04. Precedent: Rogers et al. (1999) for the effect; Mihailidis et al. (2008) for the same outcome measure in a prompting system.|EM-02; M-02, M-04|M-02 mean above the same residents' pre-System baseline, with no increase in M-04.|3-8
|UC01-F2-CL2|F2|OBJ-03, OBJ-01|Positive. Where the resident does stall, the graduated ladder resolves it at a lower level of assistance than current practice, which moves to naming or demonstrating at once. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, SA-01, HV-04.|HFC-03. Precedent: Rogers et al. (1999) found significantly fewer physical assists under graduated prompting.|EM-02; M-03, M-02|M-03 distribution shifted toward lower levels than baseline, at equal or better completion on M-02.|6-8
|UC01-F2-CL3|F2|OBJ-02|Positive. Increases the resident's felt sense of doing the activity themselves rather than being led through it. Concerns ST-01, HV-01.|HFC-01; HV-01. Ryan & Deci (2000) for the mechanism; Peek et al. (2014) for acceptance factors in this population.|EM-02; M-05 (self-report and observer rating)|Both parts of M-05 are required. Holds if the observer's support-of-autonomy rating is higher than the pre-System baseline, and the self-report mean is no lower than baseline. The observer rating carries the Claim; the self-report alone cannot, because resident self-reports show documented ceiling effects (Custers et al., 2013; Open item O-8). If the two disagree, report both and treat the Claim as unresolved.|3-8
|UC01-F2-CL4|F2|OBJ-02, OBJ-03|**Adverse.** For residents whose current ability falls below a step's demand, the deliberate wait before prompting increases observed frustration rather than producing independent completion. Concerns ST-01, HV-05.|HFC-03; HV-05. This tests VT-03 directly. Precedent: mistimed and false-alarm prompts in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008) were observed to produce general frustration and confusion - the same mechanism CL1 relies on, running the wrong way.|EM-02; M-04, M-03|Holds if M-04 (Resistiveness to Care Scale) rises above baseline for the lower-ability subgroup while M-02 does not improve for them. If it holds, the wait interval must become per-resident rather than configured once.|4, 6
|UC01-F2-CL5|F2|OBJ-05|**Adverse.** Being prompted through tooth brushing by a robot is experienced as infantilising, independently of whether it improves completion. Concerns ST-01, HV-02.|HFC-04, HFC-05; HV-02. Kitwood (1997), who names infantilisation and outpacing among the detractors that erode personhood in dementia care. Robot-specific: Lee et al. (2016) on ageing stereotypes in assistive robot design; Coghlan et al. (2021), whose older participants (living at home, no dementia) called companion robots "patronizing" when they were toy-like or imitated human companionship; and Furlanis et al. (2026), whose "embodied treatment" dimension covers the gaze, timing and tone F2 controls - including a case of exaggerated gentleness toward older adults read as condescension.|EM-02, EM-03; M-04, M-06|Holds if residents (EM-02 post-session interview) or care staff (EM-03) describe the prompting as talking down to the resident or as child-like (M-06 code T), or if M-04 rises above baseline during prompting. Tests VT-04. Sensitive to §1.3: Pemberton et al. (2026) suggest an expressive humanoid form would raise this risk relative to an appliance-like one, so a negative result on one embodiment does not clear the other. The selected embodiment is Pepper (TECH-01), the higher-risk form; TECH-05 is the fallback if this Claim holds.|6-8
|UC01-F3-CL6|F3|OBJ-01, OBJ-03|Positive. Reduces the share of sessions that end incomplete, by bringing a care worker in when prompting has run out instead of the activity being abandoned. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, SA-01, PS-01.|HV-05; HFC-03|EM-02; M-02|Fewer incomplete sessions than baseline. (The time effect was split out into UC01-F3-CL9; Open item O-9.)|9, 10, 8-alt
|UC01-F3-CL9|F3|OBJ-06|Positive. Reduces the care worker's prompting time per resident during the morning round, because the System carries the waiting and the lower prompt levels and the care worker is called only when needed. Concerns ST-02, SA-03, HV-06.|HV-06; HFC-03, HFC-06. At risk: Rogers et al. (1999) found graduated prompting took considerably //more// caregiver time than usual care - absorbing that time is the point of the System, but it is an empirical question. Tests VT-07.|EM-02, EM-03; M-07|M-07 total care-worker prompting time per session below the pre-System baseline, with completion (M-02, CL6) no worse. Holds only if both are true: saving time by letting sessions go unfinished does not count.|9, 8-alt
|UC01-F1-CL7|F1|OBJ-04|Positive. The resident does not experience the step tracking as being watched, because it requires nothing of them and observes the activity rather than the person. Concerns ST-01, HV-03.|HFC-02, HFC-05; HV-03. Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015) - residents can experience surveillance technology as supporting autonomy, but only within a person-centred approach. Pemberton et al. (2026) sharpens the risk: older adults assigned humanoid robots the "Warden" role - oversight without attachment - which is this Claim failing by design rather than by accident.|EM-02, EM-03; M-06|No resident expressions of being watched in M-06 coding (code W, EM-02 post-session interview), and care staff judge the retained record proportionate (EM-03). Sensitive to §1.3 on the same axis as CL5: Pepper's cameras are disabled and covered, but physically present.|1, 3
|UC01-F4-CL8|F4|OBJ-04, OBJ-06|Positive. The three-item session record gives the care worker enough to adjust the support they offer, without exposing how the resident performed a personal-care activity step by step. Concerns ST-02, ST-01, HV-03.|HV-03; VT-02 resolution; Niemeijer et al. (2010) on proportionality of what is recorded.|EM-03; M-06|Care staff report the record sufficient for handover and identify nothing in it they consider intrusive.|11
|UC01-F2-CL10|F2|OBJ-01|Positive. The early-stop offer at SA-01 task 7 makes the resident brush for longer than at baseline, and so leaves less plaque. Concerns ST-01, SA-01, and PS-01's completion sub-problem (stopping brushing too early).|HFC-03; HV-05, HV-04. Group reasoning from PS-01's completion sub-problem. Seuntjens et al. (2025) support the step from longer brushing to less plaque, in single brushing exercises outside this population. Nearest precedent: in MOUTh (Jablonski et al., 2018), a relationship-based intervention for nursing-home residents with dementia who resisted mouth care, care staff achieved longer mouth care than controls (d = 0.56) but only a small differential improvement in oral health (d = 0.18). Care staff delivered that mouth care, so no published precedent has been found for prompting the resident to brush longer themselves (Open item O-23). MOUTh's small oral-health effect is also why this Claim requires plaque to fall, and not brushing time alone.|EM-02; M-08, M-04, M-06|Holds if brushing time (M-08 part b) is above the same residents' baseline //and// plaque (M-08 part a) is below it, with no rise in M-04 and no T codes about the brushing offer in M-06. If brushing time rises and plaque does not fall, the Claim is unsupported, since time spent is only a proxy for how well the teeth were cleaned.|7, 3-alt
|UC01-F3-CL11|F3|OBJ-01, OBJ-06|**Adverse.** Because the care worker is now absent for most of the routine, brushing quality falls even where independent step completion rises. The System counts SA-01 task 7 as done once brushing motion has occurred, while a care worker standing beside the resident would also have seen how well they brushed. Concerns ST-01, ST-02, HV-05.|HV-05; VT-07, VT-08. Raised by the midterm feedback, which asked how well the teeth were actually brushed. M-02 cannot detect this effect, because it scores step completion only.|EM-02; M-08, M-02|Holds if plaque (M-08 part a) is higher than the same residents' baseline during the System phase. If it holds while UC01-F2-CL1 also holds, the System is trading oral health for independence. The first response is to raise B-target for those residents; the second is a periodic brushing check by the care worker, outside the System.|7, 9

