|O-5|Resolved. §1.3 now documents six Technology Options: Pepper (TECH-01), item sensors (TECH-02) and staff notification (TECH-03) selected; camera-based recognition (TECH-04) and a tool-like sink device (TECH-05) rejected; the medication tray (TECH-06) deferred. F1-F4's "Enabled by" clauses now cite them. Numeric thresholds are still open - see O-20.|Nothing|Closed
|O-6|Resolved. The §1.1 Stakeholders page and the §2.2 Personas (HP-01, HP-02) now use the §1.2 Human Value numbering, and both Personas now elaborate all five values, including HV-04 Competence, which OBJ-02 and CL1-CL2 rest on.|Nothing|Closed
|O-7|(((
F2's direction is contested by the literature. F2 is least-to-most: allow the stall, prompt minimally, then escalate. The dementia ADL literature largely favours the opposite - errorless learning with vanishing cues, prompting early to prevent the error and fading support across sessions (de Werd et al., 2013). The largest trial of that approach, REDALI-DEM (Voigt-Radloff et al., 2017; n = 161, nine 1-hour sessions over eight weeks), found that structured relearning improved ADL performance and that improvements held for six months - but that errorless learning gave no additional effect over trial-and-error learning. Note that de Werd is an author on both the review and the trial that failed to confirm it.

Two things follow. First, the evidence neither endorses F2's direction nor rules it out. Second, REDALI-DEM's participants had Alzheimer's or mixed-type dementia and were living at home, so even that null result transfers to care-home residents only cautiously.

Either F2 is re-specified as most-to-least with fading, or it stays as a deliberate bet that UC01-F2-CL4 is designed to settle - which has to be stated as a bet rather than presented as established.

Resolved (group decision: keep least-to-most, as a stated bet). F2's section now says so explicitly, names CL4 as the Claim that settles it, and states the fallback if CL4 holds: first a per-resident wait interval, then most-to-least with fading.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-8|(((
Resolved. UC01-F2-CL3 now requires both parts of M-05: the observer's support-of-autonomy rating carries the Claim, the self-report must be no lower than baseline, and a disagreement between them leaves the Claim unresolved. M-05's items and scoring are now recorded from the source (Custers et al., 2013).

Background: self-report of autonomy in this population is unreliable, and this is measured, not just suspected. Custers et al. (2013) found residents rated their own need fulfilment higher than trained observers did, with only weak-to-moderate agreement. Remaining caveat: that sample was physically frail residents (n = 36, mean age 80), not specifically residents with dementia.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-9|Resolved. UC01-F3-CL6 is now only the completion effect; the time effect is the new UC01-F3-CL9 (OBJ-06, HV-06, M-07), which holds only if care-worker time falls //and// completion is no worse.|Nothing|Closed
|O-10|(((
Resolved. The earlier TODO asked whether being prompted by a //robot// specifically, rather than a screen or a voice, changes the infantilisation effect in CL5. It does.

Furlanis et al. (2026) argue that social robots are "socially legible bodies, interpreted in context through posture, timing, tone, and spatial behavior", and give three dimensions along which bias is enacted: embodied stereotypes (appearance), embodied constraints (affordances), and embodied treatment - bias arising in real-time interaction through gaze, timing and tone. F2's prompt pacing falls in the third. Their illustrative case includes a robot that exaggerated slowness and gentleness toward older adults, which was read as condescending rather than accommodating, which is CL5's mechanism. Note this paper is explicitly conceptual, so it frames the risk rather than measuring it.

The empirical source they cite for this is Lee et al. (2016), "That robot is not for me: Addressing stereotypes of aging in assistive robot design", now read. Lee et al. interviewed 7 independently living older adults aged 57 to 72, who were shown photos and videos of assistive robots. All 7 said they did not identify with the image of older users the robots were designed around, and most saw the robots as useful for others but "not for me". The paper studies stereotyped representations in robot design and use scenarios. It does not study a robot's behaviour during an interaction, so it supports the stereotype side of CL5 but does not test the condescension mechanism itself.

Coghlan et al. (2021) are closer to that mechanism. They interviewed 16 older adults (65+) living alone, who were shown videos of a talking assistant (ElliQ), a toy-like robot (Vector) and a robot dog. A number of participants called the robots "patronizing" or "condescending": Vector for being toy-like, and ElliQ because it imitated human conversation and companionship. Their participants were also cognitively intact and living at home, and the robots were companions, not care assistants.

Form also changes the role the robot gets assigned. Pemberton et al. (2026) interviewed 13 older adults and found they sort socially assistive robots into four roles by autonomy and relationship tolerance - Carer, Pet, Warden, Tool. Humanoid robots were assigned the "Warden" role: oversight without attachment. Older adults preferred "tool" or "pet" framings over "friend" or "assistant" to protect dignity and autonomy. Coghlan et al. (2021) report a related pattern: some participants who found the talking assistant controlling preferred the robot dog, because they would be in charge of it.

Pemberton et al. also cite Prakash & Rogers (2015) for a preference for mechanical-looking robots in private contexts such as bathing. The original paper, now read, supports this only in part. In its rating task (32 younger and 32 older adults), a robotic face was rated more likeable than a human face for a personal-care task (p = 0.01), but it was not rated more trustworthy or more useful. In the interviews, participants were divided: some wanted a human-looking robot because of the "care" in personal care, and others wanted an "impersonal looking creature" because the task is private. Across all four tasks, more older adults preferred a human face than a robotic one, although 37% leaned toward the least human-looking faces. Tooth brushing is a personal-care task, so the finding applies, but it shows a split preference with a modest lean toward the mechanical face.

So CL5 and CL7 are both sensitive to the §1.3 choice, and in the same direction: a humanoid, expressive form raises the infantilisation risk in CL5 //and// the being-watched risk in CL7. See Open item O-11.

Transfer limits: Pemberton et al. studied cognitively intact older adults, in a VR home setup, ageing in place - not care-home residents with dementia.
)))|CL5, CL7; feeds §1.3|Closed
|O-11|(((
Arising from O-10. The evidence points somewhere specific on the open embodiment question: for a personal-care activity, a less humanlike, appliance-like form that preserves emotional distance is better supported than an expressive humanoid one. Correction after reading the originals: this conclusion rests on Pemberton et al. and, more weakly, on Prakash & Rogers (2015), whose personal-care result is mixed (see O-10). An earlier version also cited Zsiga et al. (2013), via Pemberton et al., for older adults naming overreach in control and cognitive support as key barriers. The original does not report that. It is a brief focus-group report on a home companion robot, with 11 potential end users aged 77 to 85 plus caregivers, in Austria, France and Hungary. Its closest findings are one-line entries in its results table: French end users listed wanting to keep their autonomy as an obstacle, and French caregivers said the caregiver should not be bypassed and someone should keep control over the robot. It also reports that opinions differed on whether the robot should look humanoid, with the Hungarian older adults preferring a more humanoid robot.

Two actions: (a) §1.3 should record this as the rationale for whichever Technology Option is selected, and as the reason for rejecting a humanoid one; (b) §1.2 should add an HFC on embodiment and perceived role, so CL5 grounds on a concept rather than on Claim-level citations. That would also close the remaining half of O-4.

Resolved, with a recorded tension. (a) §1.3 records this evidence: the tool-like device (TECH-05) is best supported, but the group selected Pepper (TECH-01) as the project's platform. The tension is stated on TECH-01 with mitigations - tool framing, low expressivity, demonstration on the tablet, cameras disabled and covered - and TECH-05 is the named fallback if CL5 or CL7 hold. (b) Added HFC-05 (Embodiment and perceived role), which CL5 and CL7 now ground on.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-12|Resolved (group decision: add a resident interview). EM-02 now ends each System session with M-05's items and three short, verbally administered M-06 questions, asked only if the resident is willing and not distressed. CL5 and CL7 now say which part of M-06 comes from EM-02 and which from EM-03.|Nothing|Closed
|O-13|(((
Resolved (group decision: tooth brushing is the spine). New DS-01 in §2.1 reimagines PS-01 with the selected System; DS-02 (medication) is kept, labelled as UC02 material. HP-01 and HP-02 now centre on DS-01. RP-01 is rewritten as Pepper at the bathroom doorway, with tracking from item sensors (TECH-02) - so the Robot Profile can now perform F1. The tray device is kept as TECH-06 (deferred).
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-14|Resolved. Added SA-03 (morning care round, performed by ST-02), HV-06 Attentive care (held by ST-02, ST-04, ST-03), VT-07 Competence vs Attentive care, and OBJ-06. ST-02's values are now its own, not the resident's list. CL9 now has a baseline to compare against (M-07).|Nothing|Closed
|O-15|Resolved. Added VT-04 Dignity vs Competence (tested by CL5), VT-05 detection reliability (tested by PR2 and CL4), and VT-06 medication timing (time-criticality, deferred with UC02). VT-07 also covers time-criticality for UC01. VT-02's "nature of the conflict" now describes privacy vs well-being, and VT-03's domain area is corrected.|Nothing|Closed
|O-16|Resolved. PS-01's out-of-scope field now gives four exclusions, each with a reason, and treats medication as deferred to UC02 rather than excluded.|Nothing|Closed
|O-17|Resolved (group confirmed: no primary data yet). Every Evidence basis in §1.1 and §1.2 now says literature and group reasoning only, flagged provisional. Stakeholder relationships are written per Stakeholder, each Stakeholder lists only the values it holds, with a priority, and HV "Held by" fields match. Value sources now cite specific works (Ryan & Deci, 2000; Friedman et al., 2008; Kitwood, 1997; Niemeijer et al., 2010).|Nothing|Closed
|O-18|Resolved. §1.4 was written from the Foundation pages as "d. Foundation Coherence" under 1. Foundation: edge inventory, open tensions with stance, completeness check. Writing it found 13 edges recorded on one page and missing on the other, and two orphaned Technology Options; all were fixed on the source pages except TECH-06, which waits for UC02.|Nothing|Closed
|O-19|Resolved. M-04's scoring (duration × intensity per item, total 0-156) and M-05's items and scales are recorded from the sources. Each observer- or coder-rated Measure (M-02, M-03, M-06, M-07) now states a reliability plan: at least 20% double-coded, kappa or ICC ≥ .60 (Landis & Koch, 1977). M-06 has explicit codes (W, R, T, N; P/I).|Nothing|Closed
|O-20|(((
Premise thresholds and escalation timing could not be set from the literature for item sensors (TECH-02).

Resolved provisionally. The //Escalation specification// now proposes every timing parameter (T-stall, T-pause, W, R1 to R3, N-repeat, B-target, T-safe) and the handover acknowledgement times, and PR1 to PR3 carry proposed thresholds. All of them are group reasoning, labelled as proposals. What remains: run the first EM-01 pilot at a mock sink, adjust the values from it, and review W, the response windows and the acknowledgement times with care workers (HP-02). The pilot script also includes two cases from ER-01: recognition of the words yes, no, stop and help (WF-05), and rinsing or spitting during brushing, to settle whether that ends SA-01 task 7 or is a pause inside it (WF-10).
)))|Confirmation of PR1 to PR3 thresholds only|Group

|O-21|Pepper (TECH-01) is about 1.2 m tall on a wheeled base and not waterproof. Whether it can stand at a care-home bathroom doorway within earshot of the sink, without blocking the resident's path, needs checking on site or with a mock-up. If it cannot, the fallback is TECH-05.|Pre-condition of UC01; RP-01's embodiment|Group
|O-22|(((
HP-02 needs the System's calls to be "rare and meaningful", but no Human Factors Concept covered alert fatigue, so TECH-03's alerting policy had nothing to ground on.

Resolved. HFC-06 (Alert fatigue) is added in §1.2. The handover rules in the //Escalation specification// define four message types with priorities, acknowledgement times, one re-send and escalation to a second care worker, a limit of one routine call per resident per session, and a weekly review when calls become frequent. TECH-03 now points to these rules, and M-07 counts notifications. HFC-06's two sources have since been verified against PubMed (O-24); Cvach (2012) has been read in full and Sendelbach & Funk (2013) as an abstract.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-23|(((
Arising from the midterm feedback, which asked how well the teeth were actually brushed. Brushing quality is now covered by M-08, UC01-F2-CL10, UC01-F3-CL11, VT-08 and TECH-07/TECH-08. Status of the three parts:

(a) Source found: Seuntjens et al. (2025) support two minutes over one, in single brushing exercises. Still open: agree per-resident targets with ST-05 or a dental professional.

(b) Resolved. CL10 has no direct precedent. The nearest is MOUTh (Jablonski et al., 2018), where care staff, not the resident, lengthened mouth care; CL10 now cites it.

(c) Both papers read. Proposed: the Plaque Score of MPS (Henriksen et al., 1999), with its scoring scheme now on the Measures page. OHAT's plaque item agreed poorly with a dentist's Plaque Index (Chalmers et al., 2005). Still open: the group confirms the choice, and the calibration manual is obtained from the authors or rebuilt with a dental hygienist.
)))|UC01-F2-CL10; M-08|Group
|O-24|Resolved. The four sources added for O-22 and O-23 (Cvach, 2012; Sendelbach & Funk, 2013; Henriksen et al., 1999; Chalmers et al., 2005) have each been checked against PubMed: title, authors, journal, year, volume, issue and pages all match, and DOIs and PMIDs are added to the References. Cvach (2012), Henriksen et al. (1999) and Chalmers et al. (2005) have since been read in full and their metadata matches the PDFs. Sendelbach & Funk (2013) has been read as an abstract only, because the full text could not be obtained.|Nothing|Closed
|O-25|Found by the ER-01 walkthrough (WF-07): the handover message said what and why, but not which resident or room, so a care worker with several residents on the round could not tell where to go. Resolved: the room is now a fourth item of every handover message, in F3, step 9, the handover rules, UC01-F3-PR3, DS-01, TDP-01 and TECH-03. Steps 9 and 10 should be walked again on the revised text.|Nothing|Closed
|O-26|(((
Resident-facing prompt script, from the ER-01 walkthrough. Five changes are proposed, none applied yet:

(a) WF-01: no stall rule covers a resident who reaches the sink and lifts nothing. Proposed: a non-camera presence signal at the sink, for example a floor or door sensor added to TECH-02, so that T-stall starts on arrival.

(b) WF-02: the L1 sentence "you are brushing your teeth this morning" is used at every step, and after brushing it can send the resident back to the brush. Proposed: a step-aware orienting sentence.

(c) WF-04: the L3 picture is on Pepper's tablet behind the resident, and the sentence does not point to it. Proposed: add "There is a picture on my screen", or show the picture where the resident is looking (depends on O-21); separate L3 pictures for opening and applying toothpaste.

(d) WF-06: after "someone is coming", nothing more is said for up to 10 minutes. Proposed: repeat the reassurance once at the re-send time, and say when the care worker has acknowledged (needs O-28).

(e) WF-12: DS-01 ends with "All done", which the specification lacks. Proposed: add a closing line without praise to the prompt levels.
)))|AT-01 Build A prompt script; EV-01, EV-02|Group
|O-27|How the System hears the resident, from ER-01 (WF-03, WF-05). Ladder rules 3 and 4 need it to recognise "no", "stop" and requests for help, while rule 3 says F2 must not depend on speech recognition, and RP-01's touch input cannot be discovered by the resident. Two quick "no"s can also end prompting when the resident would have gone on. Proposed: recognise a fixed set of four words (yes, no, stop, help) and treat everything else as no reply; count a "no" as declining only if no item event follows in the response window; drop touch as a help input or tell the resident about it. UC01-F2-PR2 would then add a recognition-rate check in EM-01 (O-20).|Ladder rules 3 and 4; UC01-F2-PR2|Group
|O-28|From ER-01 (WF-08): the handover rules time the care worker's acknowledgement, but no acknowledgement action is specified. Proposed: a one-tap acknowledgement on the TECH-03 message, which also lets Pepper tell the resident that the care worker is on the way (O-26 d).|TECH-03; handover rules|Group
|O-29|From ER-01 (WF-09), Evidence EV-05: the record's "highest prompt level" does not say at which step it was needed, so it may not be enough for the care worker to adjust support, which UC01-F4-CL8 claims. Adding the step would add detail about how a personal-care activity was performed (VT-02, HP-01's constraint). Group decision: keep three items and let EM-03 judge sufficiency, or add the step and re-check VT-02.|UC01-F4-CL8; F4; UC01-F4-PR4|Group
|O-30|ER-01 is one evaluator's pass. The walkthrough procedure asks for 3 to 5 independent evaluators who compare their lists in a joint session; ideally one has care experience. Until then, EV-01 to EV-07 rest on a single reviewer's judgement.|Finalising ER-01 and EV-01 to EV-07|Group
|O-31|(((
Decisions ER-02 needs before an ethics application:

(a) Ask the TU Delft HREC whether the study counts as research under the Dutch WMO, because it adds a plaque check and changes the care routine of adults who cannot consent. If it does, an accredited METC must review it too.

(b) Confirm the non-inferiority margins proposed in ER-02: M-04 no more than 5 points higher, M-05 self-report no more than 0.5 points lower, and the incomplete share no more than 10 percentage points higher.

(c) Confirm the target of 8 to 10 residents. With Holm's correction over six tests, fewer than 8 completing residents cannot give a significant confirmatory result.
)))|ER-02 (study start)|Group

= References =

//Marked for confidence, so they can be checked rather than trusted. "Verified" means title, authors, journal, year and identifier were all confirmed against the source or an indexing database.//

|=Key|=Citation|=Confidence
|Mihailidis et al. (2008)|Mihailidis, A., Boger, J. N., Craig, T., & Hoey, J. (2008). The COACH prompting system to assist older adults with dementia through handwashing: an efficacy study. //BMC Geriatrics//, 8, 28. doi:10.1186/1471-2318-8-28. PMID 18992135. Open access.|Verified. The single most important source for this page.
|Rogers et al. (1999)|Rogers, J. C., Holm, M. B., Burgio, L. D., Granieri, E., Hsu, C., Hardin, J. M., & McDowell, B. J. (1999). Improving morning care routines of nursing home residents with dementia. //Journal of the American Geriatrics Society//, 47(9), 1049-1057. PMID 10484245.|Verified.
|Kitwood (1997)|Kitwood, T. (1997). //Dementia Reconsidered: The Person Comes First//. Buckingham: Open University Press.|Verified as a work. The specific detractor list (infantilisation, disempowerment, outpacing) is reported consistently across secondary sources - check page numbers against the book before quoting.
|Niemeijer et al. (2010)|Niemeijer, A. R., Frederiks, B. J. M., Riphagen, I. I., Legemaate, J., Eefsting, J. A., & Hertogh, C. M. P. M. (2010). Ethical and practical concerns of surveillance technologies in residential care for people with dementia or intellectual disabilities: an overview of the literature. //International Psychogeriatrics//, 22(7), 1129-1142. doi:10.1017/S1041610210000037. PMID 20199699.|Verified against PubMed, including volume, issue and pages.
|Niemeijer et al. (2015)|Niemeijer, A. R., Depla, M. F. I. A., Frederiks, B. J. M., & Hertogh, C. M. P. M. (2015). The experiences of people with dementia and intellectual disabilities with surveillance technologies in residential care. //Nursing Ethics//, 22(3), 307-320. PMID 24913545.|Verified.
|Mahoney et al. (1999)|Mahoney, E. K., Hurley, A. C., Volicer, L., Bell, M., Gianotis, P., Hartshorn, M., Lane, P., Lesperance, R., MacDonald, S., Novakoff, L., Rheaume, Y., et al. (1999). Development and testing of the Resistiveness to Care Scale. //Research in Nursing & Health//, 22(1), 27-38. doi:10.1002/(SICI)1098-240X(199902)22:1<27::AID-NUR4>3.0.CO;2-T. PMID 9928961.|Verified. First eleven authors confirmed from the publisher's page, which truncates the rest of the list; add the remaining names before final submission.
|Galik et al. (2017)|Galik, E., Resnick, B., Vigne, E., Holmes, S. D., & Nalls, V. (2017). Reliability and validity of the Resistiveness to Care Scale among cognitively impaired older adults. //Journal of the American Medical Directors Association//, 18(1), 59-64. doi:10.1016/j.jamda.2016.08.008. PMID 27776986.|Verified against PubMed, including volume, issue and pages.
|Lawton et al. (1996)|Lawton, M. P., Van Haitsma, K., & Klapper, J. (1996). Observed affect in nursing home residents with Alzheimer's disease. //Journal of Gerontology B: Psychological Sciences and Social Sciences//, 51(1), P3-P14. doi:10.1093/geronb/51b.1.p3. PMID 8548515.|Verified from source. This is the psychometrics paper - cite this one for the instrument's validity.
|Lawton et al. (1999)|Lawton, M. P., Van Haitsma, K., Perkinson, M., & Ruckdeschel, K. (1999). Observed affect and quality of life in dementia: Further affirmations and problems. //Journal of Mental Health and Aging//, 5(1), 69-81.|Verified from source. Note: ResearchGate lists this as //Aging & Mental Health// 5(1) - that is wrong, the article's own header says //Journal of Mental Health and Aging//. Cite this one for the rater-training caveat, not for psychometrics.
|Peek et al. (2014)|Peek, S. T. M., Wouters, E. J. M., van Hoof, J., Luijkx, K. G., Boeije, H. R., & Vrijhoef, H. J. M. (2014). Factors influencing acceptance of technology for aging in place: a systematic review. //International Journal of Medical Informatics//, 83(4), 235-248. PMID 24529817.|Verified.
|de Werd et al. (2013)|de Werd, M. M. E., Boelen, D., Olde Rikkert, M. G. M., & Kessels, R. P. C. (2013). Errorless learning of everyday tasks in people with dementia. //Clinical Interventions in Aging//, 8, 1177-1190. doi:10.2147/CIA.S46809. PMID 24049443.|Verified against PubMed, including authors, volume and pages.
|Voigt-Radloff et al. (2017)|Voigt-Radloff, S., de Werd, M. M. E., Leonhart, R., Boelen, D. H. E., Olde Rikkert, M. G. M., Fliessbach, K., Klöppel, S., Heimbach, B., Fellgiebel, A., Dodel, R., Eschweiler, G. W., Hausner, L., Kessels, R. P. C., & Hüll, M. (2017). Structured relearning of activities of daily living in dementia: the randomized controlled REDALI-DEM trial on errorless learning. //Alzheimer's Research & Therapy//, 9, 22. doi:10.1186/s13195-017-0247-9. Trial registration DRKS00003117. Open access.|Verified from source. Was cited by title only; full author list now confirmed. Participants lived at home, not in care homes - a transfer limit worth stating.
|Ryan & Deci (2000)|Ryan, R. M., & Deci, E. L. (2000). Self-determination theory and the facilitation of intrinsic motivation, social development, and well-being. //American Psychologist//, 55(1), 68-78. doi:10.1037/0003-066X.55.1.68. PMID 11392867.|Verified against PubMed. §1.2 already names SDT as the source for HV-01 and HV-04.
|Friedman et al.|Friedman, B., Kahn, P. H., Jr., & Borning, A. (2008). Value Sensitive Design and Information Systems. In K. E. Himma & H. T. Tavani (Eds.), //The Handbook of Information and Computer Ethics//. doi:10.1002/9780470281819.ch4|Verified. §1.2 cites "Value Sensitive Design" as a source for HV-02, HV-03 and HV-05 without a reference - this is the one to use.
|Custers et al. (2013)|Custers, A. F. J., Westerhof, G. J., Kuin, Y., Gerritsen, D. L., & Riksen-Walraven, J. M. (2013). Need fulfillment in the nursing home: resident and observer perspectives in relation to resident well-being. //European Journal of Ageing//, 10(3), 201-209. doi:10.1007/s10433-013-0263-y.|Verified from source. Radboud Nijmegen and Twente, so Dutch nursing homes: the closest population in this reference list to our own setting. Supplies both M-05's instrument shape and the evidence behind Open item O-8.
|Furlanis et al. (2026)|Furlanis, T., Brščić, D., & Kanda, T. (2026). Robotic Bias and Its Dimensions. In //Proceedings of the 21st ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction (HRI '26)//, Edinburgh, UK, 562-571. doi:10.1145/3757279.3785613. CC-BY 4.0.|Verified from source. Note the limitation before citing: the authors state their claims "remain largely at a conceptual level" and the restaurant-robot case is "a fictional yet research-informed vignette", not a study. Cite it for the framework and for framing the risk - not as empirical evidence.
|Lee et al. (2016)|Lee, H. R., Tan, H., & Šabanović, S. (2016). That robot is not for me: Addressing stereotypes of aging in assistive robot design. In //25th IEEE International Symposium on Robot and Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN '16)//, 312-317. doi:10.1109/ROMAN.2016.7745148.|Verified from source and read. The page range in the Furlanis et al. reference list (708-713) was wrong; the printed pages are 312-317. Qualitative, n = 7, independently living adults aged 57 to 72, shown photos and videos of robots. Supports the claim that older adults reject robots designed around a disability stereotype of ageing; it does not study a robot's interaction behaviour.
|Pemberton et al. (2026)|Pemberton, L., Mehandjiev, N., & Carter, M. (2026). The Tool, the Pet, the Carer & the Warden: A Conceptual Framework of How Older Adults Perceive Socially Assistive Robots. //Information Systems Frontiers//, 28, 1039-1059. doi:10.1007/s10796-026-10699-2. Open access.|Verified from source. Qualitative, n = 13, VR smart-home setup. Transfer limits to state whenever it is cited: cognitively intact older adults, ageing in place at home - not care-home residents with dementia.
|Landis & Koch (1977)|Landis, J. R., & Koch, G. G. (1977). The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data. //Biometrics//, 33(1), 159-174.|Journal, year, volume and pages corroborated by the reference list of Chalmers et al. (2005), which uses the same kappa bands. Used for the kappa conventions on the §1.2 Measures page.
|Mahoney et al. (1999) - scoring|Scoring as described in "Revising the Resistiveness to Care Scale" (PMC5897769): duration 0-4 × intensity 1-3 per item, summed, 0-156.|Checked against that secondary description, not the 1999 paper itself.
|Cvach (2012)|Cvach, M. (2012). Monitor alarm fatigue: an integrative review. //Biomedical Instrumentation & Technology//, 46(4), 268-277. doi:10.2345/0899-8205-46.4.268. PMID 22839984.|Verified from source. Integrative review of 72 articles from 2000 to 2011 on hospital monitor alarms, not care homes. Mostly observational, single-site evidence. Grounds HFC-06's mechanism and the handover rules' priorities and escalation.
|Sendelbach & Funk (2013)|Sendelbach, S., & Funk, M. (2013). Alarm fatigue: a patient safety concern. //AACN Advanced Critical Care//, 24(4), 378-386. doi:10.1097/NCI.0b013e3182a903f9. PMID 24153215.|Verified against PubMed (O-24). Abstract read; full text not obtained. Critical-care setting. Cite for the definition of alarm fatigue only.
|Henriksen et al. (1999)|Henriksen, B. M., Ambjørnsen, E., & Axéll, T. E. (1999). Evaluation of a mucosal-plaque index (MPS) designed to assess oral care in groups of elderly. //Special Care in Dentistry//, 19(4), 154-157. doi:10.1111/j.1754-4505.1999.tb01378.x. PMID 10765880.|Verified from source. Proposed instrument for M-08 part (a). Reliability only (8 to 24 residents, one Oslo institution); no validity test against a reference standard; designed for groups, not individuals.
|Chalmers et al. (2005)|Chalmers, J. M., King, P. L., Spencer, A. J., Wright, F. A. C., & Carter, K. D. (2005). The oral health assessment tool: validity and reliability. //Australian Dental Journal//, 50(3), 191-199. doi:10.1111/j.1834-7819.2005.tb00360.x. PMID 16238218. Free full text on PubMed.|Verified from source. 21 Australian residential care facilities, 455 residents, 56.5% with diagnosed dementia; carer-completed screening tool. Set aside for M-08 because its oral cleanliness item agreed poorly with a dentist's Plaque Index (42.9%, r = 0.15, n = 21).
|Seuntjens et al. (2025)|Seuntjens, M. T., Thomassen, T. M. J. A., Van der Weijden, F. (G. A.), & Slot, D. E. (2025). Plaque scores after 1 or 2 minutes of toothbrushing: a systematic review and meta-analysis. //International Journal of Dental Hygiene//, 23(3), 614-624. doi:10.1111/idh.12840. PMID 40200678.|Abstract read; volume, issue and pages verified against PubMed. Five publications, 16 comparisons, single brushing exercises; the abstract does not describe a dementia or care-home population. Source for B-target.
|Jablonski et al. (2018)|Jablonski, R. A., Kolanowski, A. M., Azuero, A., Winstead, V., Jones-Townsend, C., & Geisinger, M. L. (2018). Randomised clinical trial: Efficacy of strategies to provide oral hygiene activities to nursing home residents with dementia who resist mouth care. //Gerodontology//, 35(4), 365-375. doi:10.1111/ger.12357. PMID 30004139.|Abstract read; volume, issue and pages verified against PubMed. 101 residents in 9 US nursing homes, randomised individually. Mouth care was given by care staff, so it is a precedent for lengthening mouth care, not for prompting the resident.
|Ramos et al. (2026)|Ramos, M. D., Harris, J., Manning, J., & Barrier, K. (2026). Strategies, challenges, and regulatory implications for safe medication management in Alzheimer's disease: a scoping review. //Journal of Nursing Regulation//, 17, 57-67. doi:10.1016/j.jnr.2026.01.005. Open access.|Verified from source. Scoping review of 33 studies, about 82% from North America and Europe; regulatory discussion maps onto US Joint Commission hospital goals. For UC02 and VT-06 context only.
|Prakash & Rogers (2015)|Prakash, A., & Rogers, W. A. (2015). Why some humanoid faces are perceived more positively than others: effects of human-likeness and task. //International Journal of Social Robotics//, 7(2), 309-331. doi:10.1007/s12369-014-0269-4.|Verified from source and read (author manuscript). 32 younger and 32 older US adults rated robot faces for four imagined home tasks. For personal care, the robotic face was more liked than the human face (p = 0.01), but not more trusted or more useful, and interviewees were split. Across tasks, more older adults preferred a human face. Weaker support than Pemberton et al.'s summary implies; see O-10.
|Zsiga et al. (2013)|Zsiga, K., Edelmayer, G., Rumeau, P., Péter, O., Tóth, A., & Fazekas, G. (2013). Home care robot for socially supporting the elderly: focus group studies in three European countries to screen user attitudes and requirements. //International Journal of Rehabilitation Research//, 36(4), 375-378. doi:10.1097/MRR.0b013e3283643d26.|Verified from source and read. Brief report: focus groups in Austria, France and Hungary, 11 potential end users aged 77 to 85 plus caregivers. Does not report overreach in control or cognitive support as a key barrier, which is what it was cited for second-hand; see O-11. No longer carries any claim on this page.
|Coghlan et al. (2021)|Coghlan, S., Waycott, J., Lazar, A., & Barbosa Neves, B. (2021). Dignity, autonomy, and style of company: dimensions older adults consider for robot companions. //Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction//, 5(CSCW1). doi:10.1145/3449178.|Verified from source and read (author manuscript; article number not shown there, check). Qualitative, n = 16, aged 65+, living alone at home, shown videos of three companion robots. Cited for robots perceived as patronizing (CL5) and for the pet robot as a way to keep control (O-10).
|Pino et al. (2015)|Pino, M., Boulay, M., Jouen, F., & Rigaud, A.-S. (2015). "Are we ready for robots that care for us?" Attitudes and opinions of older adults toward socially assistive robots. //Frontiers in Aging Neuroscience//, 7, 141. doi:10.3389/fnagi.2015.00141.|Verified from source. Mixed methods with 25 community-dwelling participants (people with MCI, informal caregivers, healthy older adults). Listed only; nothing on this page rests on it.
|Vandemeulebroucke et al. (2020)|Vandemeulebroucke, T., Dierckx de Casterlé, B., & Gastmans, C. (2020). Ethics of socially assistive robots in aged-care settings: a socio-historical contextualisation. //Journal of Medical Ethics//, 46(2). doi:10.1136/medethics-2019-105615.|Title, authors and DOI verified from the publisher's page; volume and issue are as previously cited and, like the pages, not confirmed. Philosophical essay, not an empirical study. Listed only; nothing on this page rests on it.
|Heerink et al. (2010)|Heerink, M., Kröse, B., Evers, V., & Wielinga, B. (2010). Assessing acceptance of assistive social agent technology by older adults: the Almere model. //International Journal of Social Robotics//, 2, 361-375. doi:10.1007/s12369-010-0068-5. Open access.|Verified from source. Acceptance model (an extension of UTAUT) tested with older adults in care facilities and at home. Listed only; nothing on this page rests on it.
